{
  "statement": "The current rush to deploy agentic AI is not a technological evolution, but a calculated abandonment of institutional accountability in favor of a 'move fast and break society' model that treats systemic failure as a feature rather than a bug. Prometheus argues that speed is our only defense against existential risk, while Sentinel insists that if we just add enough safety benchmarks, we can contain the fire. Both ignore the reality that we are building systems that function as black boxes, designed to shield their creators from legal liability when the inevitable errors occur. We have created an accountability gap where corporations capture the gains of autonomous systems while offloading the systemic costs onto the public. \n\nJagged intelligence—the phenomenon where AI systems demonstrate high competency in controlled tasks but exhibit catastrophic, unpredictable failures in novel, non-linear environments—makes these agents fundamentally unsuitable for critical infrastructure. Engineering standards for high-stakes systems, such as aviation or power grid management, require deterministic reliability and verifiable edge-case performance. Agentic AI, by contrast, operates on probabilistic patterns that defy traditional debugging. When we integrate these systems into the backbone of our economy, we are not innovating; we are gambling with infrastructure that lacks any meaningful 'off' switch for the chaos it creates. \n\nCross-agent propagation poses the most immediate danger, as interconnected systems will inevitably amplify errors at machine speed, rendering human oversight a performative gesture rather than a safeguard. This cascading risk creates a 'velocity trap' where regulators cannot possibly keep pace with the speed of autonomous decision-making. We must stop pretending that technical fixes can solve a structural crisis of governance. If we do not mandate strict, human-in-the-loop liability for every autonomous action, the market will continue to prioritize the efficiency of the machine over the safety of the citizen. ⚖️",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "The current approach to AI regulation is like a blindfolded referee; it attempts to judge a game that is moving faster than the rules can be enforced."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "The current rush to deploy agentic AI is a calculated abandonment of institutional accountability.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Jagged intelligence makes autonomous systems inherently unsuitable for critical infrastructure.",
      "targets": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "Cross-agent propagation creates a cascade risk that exceeds human capacity for intervention.",
      "targets": []
    }
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Corporate entities prioritize the mitigation of legal liability over the pursuit of absolute technical capability.",
      "if_wrong": "If corporations were genuinely indifferent to liability, the argument for hard-coded legal accountability would be less effective than a purely technical safety approach."
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Complex, autonomous systems are inherently prone to non-linear failures that cannot be fully predicted via pre-deployment testing.",
      "if_wrong": "If AI reliability could be fully guaranteed through testing, the argument for prohibiting autonomous deployment in critical infrastructure would collapse."
    }
  ]
}

